.TH CCOL_CREATE_CHTTPSVR_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp \- create an HTTP server with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                            clog cl,
                            char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates and initializes a new chttpsvr handle, using the memory
management procedures you supply, and automatically creates a root
router with an empty prefix.

mprocs may be NULL, in which case the function uses the default
system allocator.

cl controls the logger of the server, and it may be CLOG_INVALID:

- cl == CLOG_INVALID: the server creates its own internal logger,
  which writes only FATAL messages to stderr.
- cl != CLOG_INVALID: the server derives a new logger from cl (see
  clog_derive) and tags it with the field component=http-server.  The
  server uses and manages the derived logger; the cl handle of the
  caller does not change, and the caller keeps ownership of it.

In both cases, for the internal logger as for the derived one, the
server closes its own logger automatically when the server is
destroyed.

On failure the function sets err_str to a static diagnostic string,
when err_str is not NULL, and returns CHTTPSVR_INVALID.

chttpsvr is an opaque value handle (a uint64_t), not a pointer.
Before the library uses the server, it resolves the handle through a
table that it owns and checks a generation.  Do not cast the handle
to or from a pointer.  Compare it with CHTTPSVR_INVALID, which is 0,
so a truth test such as if (!srv) also works.  The library refuses a
call with the handle of a destroyed server, and where
chttpsvr_destroy(3) says so, such a call is fatal; it never causes a
use-after-free.

There are two supported ways to run more than one process that serves
traffic with this library.  The first is to fork(2) before the
process creates and starts any server: each resulting process, parent
and child alike, then calls ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp and
chttpsvr_start on its own and builds its own server from scratch.
The second is a fork(2) immediately followed by an exec(3), the
pattern behind popen, behind system, and behind launching a
subprocess from inside a request handler.  This is fully supported
whether or not a server is running at that moment, because the
program that exec(3) starts replaces the process image completely and
never touches anything this library left behind.  A process that
continues to run with no exec(3) in between, while a server that it
created before the fork is still live, is not a supported pattern.

On top of that, the library adds one defense-in-depth measure: a
fresh call to ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp, chttpsvr_start, chttpsvr_stop,
chttpsvr_destroy, chttpsvr_register_handler,
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler, chttpsvr_use or
chttpsvr_subrouter never hangs on a lock that some other thread held
at the instant of the fork, a thread that may no longer exist because
fork(2) duplicates only the calling thread.  This holds for a call
from any thread, in the parent and in a newly forked child with no
exec(3) after it.  The measure also covers a fork that lands in the
middle of a start, a stop or a destroy of that exact handle, and a
fork in the middle of a shutdown of the whole shared engine that
chttpsvr_engine_stop() drives.

A server that is live and actively running work at the moment of such
a fork is a different matter, which this measure does not change: such
a server is explicitly out of scope.  Its reactor threads, its
worker-pool threads and its idle-timeout-sweep threads do not carry
over into the child, so a server that the child inherits, and that
was already started, can no longer accept new connections there or
process in-flight work there.  Calling chttpsvr_stop() and then
chttpsvr_start() on that exact handle in the child does not bring it
back either: the call reports ccol_success, but the server still
cannot serve, because it reuses the engine reference it already held
before the fork instead of building a new one.  A caller that needs
none of this protection can compile it out with
-DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A valid chttpsvr handle on success, or CHTTPSVR_INVALID on failure.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_create_chttpsvr (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR clog_derive (3)
